<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Apple's privacy manifest. REQUIRED since May 2024 for any app that calls a "required reason"
  API; App Store Connect's binary scan flags the upload without it.

  Placement: this is the APP-level manifest, and `ios/project.yml`'s `sources: - path: App`
  already sweeps this folder, so it needs no entry of its own. A per-package manifest is for
  DISTRIBUTED third-party SDKs; `BiyaherongUI` is a first-party local package, so this file
  covers its `UserDefaults` use as well as the app target's.

  Only ONE of Apple's five required-reason categories applies here. Checked, not assumed:
    • UserDefaults      — YES. EngineSettings, CoachStore, LoginStore, OpeningTreeStore,
                          AnalysisEngineSettings and KeychainStorage all read or write it.
    • FileTimestamp     — no. Nothing reads creationDate/modificationDate.
    • DiskSpace         — no. Nothing reads volumeAvailableCapacity.
                          (`Diagnostics.swift` reads `.fileSizeKey` on one bundled file, which is
                          the size of a known file, not free space — a different API and not on
                          Apple's list.)
    • SystemBootTime    — no.
    • ActiveKeyboards   — no.

  Nothing is collected and nothing is tracked, because nothing leaves the device: the app makes
  no network calls of its own. The two Apple frameworks that do talk to Apple — StoreKit for the
  subscription and AuthenticationServices for Sign in with Apple — handle their own data under
  Apple's own manifest, not this one. `tools/qa/replay_login.js` and `replay_premium.js` fail the
  build if a URLSession or a third URL ever appears, so that claim stays a test, not a comment.

  See docs/app-store-readiness.md.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>NSPrivacyTracking</key>
	<false/>
	<key>NSPrivacyTrackingDomains</key>
	<array/>
	<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes</key>
	<array/>
	<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes</key>
	<array>
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryUserDefaults</string>
			<!--
			  CA92.1 — "Access info from the app itself, and from app group containers the app is
			  a member of." That is exactly this app: every read and write is its own settings,
			  its own saved games and its own session key. There is no app group, no SDK reading
			  it, and no value derived from another app.
			-->
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
			<array>
				<string>CA92.1</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
